We demonstrate that the decay width ratios $\Gamma(B_c^- 	o D^{(*)} \ell  ar{
u})/\Gamma(B^- 	o D^{(*)} \ell  ar{
u})$ and $\Gamma(B_c^- 	o B^{(*)} \ell  ar{
u})/\Gamma(B^- 	o B^{(*)} \ell  ar{
u})$ significantly exceed their heavy quark limit predictions of $1$ and $4/3$, respectively. This deviation arises because the heavy quark limit inherently assumes the light quark's mass to be negligible, an assumption that fails for the light quark in $B_c$ mesons. Consequently, these findings suggest that $B_c$ semileptonic decays are valuable for testing heavy quark expansion predictions.
